        "hypothesis": "A node restarted with RestoreCommittedLogs keeps the snapshot's configuration when committed membership changes sit below the restored commit index, so its quorum arithmetic reflects an obsolete membership and it can win an election its committed configuration would forbid.",
        "discriminator": "Grow a snapshot-age three-voter cluster to five voters, restart the leader, compare the configuration index and server count with the log's latest configuration, then isolate the restarted node with one committed voter and check whether it wins with the smaller quorum while the majority side keeps a leader.",
        "disposition": "active",
        "parent_id": null,
        "knowledge": null,
        "resume_conditions": null
      }
    ],
    "knowledge": [
      {
        "id": "d734f2f56cd04266808962001dc93ccf-knowledge-1",
        "ref": null,
        "kind": "behavior",
        "statement": "With Config.RestoreCommittedLogs enabled and no snapshot, NewRaft replays the committed log by pushing one fsmMutateCh message per MaxAppendEntries-sized batch before the FSM goroutine is started; when the committed backlog requires more batches than the queue's 128 slots, the send blocks and NewRaft never returns, so the node never starts.",
        "conditions": "Config.RestoreCommittedLogs=true with a CommitTrackingLogStore, no snapshot covering the committed range, and a restored commit index whose replay needs more than 128 batches of MaxAppendEntries entries (DefaultConfig sets MaxAppendEntries 64 and TrailingLogs 10240, so about 8192 retained entries; MaxAppendEntries is validated positive and at most 1024, so the boundary scales with the configured value); observed for 8402 entries (132 batches) with a bounded 15 second wait, against 8002 entries (126 batches) returning in 0-3 ms.",
        "source_ids": [
          "api_restore_committed_body",
          "api_fsm_mutate_buffer",
          "api_goroutines_after_restore",
          "raft_processlogs_applybatch",
